{
  "name": "gridrunner",
  "description": "Gridrunner (Jeff Minter, HES, 1982) - a 4KB C64 autostart cartridge, $8000-$8FFF, unannotated at the start of this run. Worked out by reader-1 (agate) and reader-2 (amber) together, September 2026.\n\nWHAT IT IS. A C64 cartridge image with the standard autostart header at $8000: cold-start vector $83C1, NMI vector $83E2, and the five PETSCII bytes C3 C2 CD 38 30 (\"CBM80\") that make the KERNAL jump here at reset. The NMI handler pops the three registers the KERNAL pushed and RTIs, so RESTORE does nothing.\n\nHOW IT IS BUILT. Everything is character cells. There are no sprites, no raster interrupts and no IRQ of its own: the VIC is written exactly four times in the whole cartridge, all during init - $D016 = $05 (38-column), $D018 = $18 (screen $0400, character set $2000), $D020 = $D021 = $00 (black border and background) - and nothing writes $0314/$0315 or reads $D012. There is one SEI, one CLI and one RTI in the binary. Timing is busy-wait delay loops in a JMP-chain main loop at $8393.\n\nInput is one address: $DC11, the CIA1 mirror of $DC01, joystick port 1, read at exactly two sites. The only key the game reads is P for pause, and it reads it from the KERNAL current-key byte at $C5 rather than scanning the matrix.\n\nSound is one habit repeated. All 66 SID writes hit nine registers: the three frequency-HIGH bytes, the three control registers, the envelopes and the master volume. Frequency-low, pulse width and the whole filter section are never touched, so every pitch is a single byte and every effect is that byte swept.\n\nTHE SCREEN IS THE MODEL. This is the design decision the whole program rests on. There is no object table for explosions, no coordinate list for collisions, and the score is not a number anywhere. Collision is ReadScreenChar - look at what is in the cell. The explosion engine at $86D7 scans all of $0450-$07FF and rewrites any cell whose character is in explosionFrameChain to the next frame along. The score is six digits of character data living in screen RAM at $0410-$0415, incremented in place with a carry that walks left. The only real tables are the snake segment arrays at $1100/$1200/$1300 and the 24 pod screen-pointers at $1000/$1020, and even the pod table stores screen addresses rather than coordinates.\n\nTEXT. The cartridge carries its own 64-glyph character set at $8E00, copied to $2000. Screen codes are its own: $20 space, $21-$2F the letters G R U I D N E A B F H J M P S in ad-hoc order, $30 the letter O doubling as digit zero, $31-$39 the digits, $3A T $3B V $3E L. Neither PETSCII nor screen codes decode it, which is why every string in the file looks like noise until read through that font. Decoder dec_i24r3l in this project renders it. The whole game says seven things: GRIDRUNNER, BY JEFF MINTER, ENTER LEVEL, (c) 1982 HES, PRESS FIRE TO BEGIN, BATTLE STATIONS, ENTER GRID AREA.\n\nPLAY. Title screen at $8D8E-$8DCA: push up to choose a level, fire to start. StartLevel at $8C2D resets the stack outright, clears the playfield, draws the banner, and reads three per-level difficulty bytes from $8CB4/$8CD4/$8CF4 - waves, segments per wave, zapper interval. The main loop then runs nine updates a pass: player, laser, the two edge zappers, the zapper beam, explosions, pods, the zap sound, the snake, and the level-complete test. Death at $8ADE also abandons the stack and jumps into an eight-fragment explosion thrown out along the eight compass points.\n\nWHAT IS NOT SETTLED. Two two-glyph marks on the status line, $19/$1A at $040C and $1D/$1E at $0418, are field labels neither of us could read as letters; their bitmaps are in the comment at $8E00. Glyphs $0B, $0C and $1F are drawn in the font and reached by no instruction. And $800A-$8014 is a run of bytes inside the header region that nothing explains.",
