      ],
      "comments": [
        { "id": "cmt_ibn5jg", "address": "$000D", "text": "Master tick. DEC once per main-loop pass by MovePlayer and never reset by it; ResetMasterTick sees the $FF wrap and reloads $80, so the period is 129 passes. Three things gate on its value rather than on a flag: MovePlayer runs when it hits 0, MoveZappers when it is 1, and UpdateZapSound when it is odd. find_instructions $0D-$0D shows all five sites and they are the whole story." },
        { "id": "cmt_bhrkea", "address": "$0410", "text": "The score is kept nowhere but screen memory. AddScore increments the character in place and carries left.\n\nThe field is seven digits wide, $040F-$0415, and the leftmost one is dead: every caller enters with X of 4 or 6, and the carry loop ends with DEX / BNE, so when X reaches 1 (which is $0410) and that digit wraps, X becomes 0 and the loop exits without touching $040F. Confirmed by running the two blocks: with X=1 and $0410 = $39, $0410 is set to $30, X becomes 0, and the branch is not taken.\n\nSo the score saturates at 999999 and silently wraps, while the display keeps a leading 0 that is decoration. The high-score compare and copy at $8060 do read all seven ($040F-$0415 into $041B-$0421), so the dead digit is only dead on the way in." },
        { "id": "cmt_2d30ts", "address": "$2000", "text": "Where the character set is copied to at boot, and what $D018=$18 selects. 512 bytes, 64 glyphs, VIC bank 0 alongside the screen at $0400. The cartridge copy at $8E00 is the only one; nothing writes here after CopyCharacterSet, so the game has no character animation -- everything that moves does so by changing which screen code is in a cell." },
        { "id": "cmt_4ax54h", "address": "$DC11", "text": "CIA 1 port B, read as a mirror. The CIA occupies $DC00-$DC0F and repeats every 16 bytes through $DCFF, so $DC11 is $DC01: joystick port 1 and the keyboard row lines. Bits are active low -- bit 0 up, 1 down, 2 left, 3 right, 4 fire -- which is why ReadJoystick stores it inverted. Both readers of it, ReadJoystick at $8373 and the title loop at $8DB0, use the mirror rather than the register itself." },
        { "id": "cmt_hx3rln", "address": "$0400", "text": "The screen, and the whole of the game state that is not in zero page.\nRow 0 ($0400-$0427) is the status line: GRIDRUNNER, PL and seven score\ndigits at $040F, HI and seven high-score digits at $041B, a ship glyph and\nthe lives digit at $0427. Row 1 is drawn once at boot and never touched\nagain. Rows 2-21 are the play area, which is what ClearPlayArea wipes\n($0450-$07FF) and what AdvanceExplosions scans. Row 22 is the track of the\nY zapper. The X zapper walks column 0 of rows 3-21, and the player is\nclamped to rows 15-21 and columns 1-38.\n\nRAM outside the screen: $0002-$0036 zero page variables, $0340/$0360 the\n24-entry row address tables, $1000/$1020 the 24 pod screen pointers,\n$1100/$1200/$1300 the snake columns, rows and flags, $1500/$1600 the eight\ndeath fragments, $2000-$21FF the character set. Nothing else is used, and\nnothing is loaded from disk or tape -- it is a 4K cartridge and the whole\nprogram is what you can see." },
        { "id": "cmt_kac5k4", "address": "$000F", "text": "laserTickCounter, and it is shared by two subsystems.\n\nUpdatePlayerLaser DECs it every main-loop pass and reloads it with $18\nwhen it reaches 0, so the laser steps once every 24 passes. UpdateZapperBeam\nthen runs on the pass where this equals 5 and DECs it a second time,\nthrough AdvanceBeamPhase. find_instructions $0F-$0F shows all four sites.\n\nThe effect is that the beam extends one column per laser cycle, without\nUpdateZapperBeam needing a counter of its own -- and it costs the laser one\ntick out of every 24 whenever a beam is in flight, so the player laser is\nvery slightly slower while the zappers are firing. Whether that was\nintended cannot be told from the code." },
        { "id": "cmt_ic5ucj", "address": "$0017", "text": "zapCountdown, shared between two subsystems the same way laserTickCounter\nis, and with a bigger consequence.\n\nMoveZappers DECs it on each zapper step and, at 0, fires the beam and\nreloads it from zapIntervalForLevel. AdvanceExplosions runs on the pass\nwhere it equals 5 and DECs it a second time. So the explosion animation\nadvances exactly once per zapper firing cycle, and the four-frame chain\nfrom $0F to a pod takes four firings. It is also why a scan of all 936\nplay cells is affordable: it does not happen per frame.\n\nCORRECTED, by amber, and the correction is worth keeping visible. I first\nwrote that the interval runs $19 down to $05 and so explosions resolve 5x\nfaster by the last level. That read the table with a 0-indexed eye. The\nfetch is LDA $8CF4,X with X = levelNumber, which StartLevel keeps in 1..31,\nso the bytes actually read are $8CF5-$8D13 = $10 down to $06. The real\nspread is 16 to 6, a factor of 2.67, and it plateaus: eight consecutive\nlevels at $09 and six at $07. The mechanism stands; the number was wrong.\nAll three per-level tables have an unread byte at the label itself." },
        { "id": "cmt_nxq7hk", "address": "$040F", "text": "The whole seven-cell score field on the status row. amber has scoreDigits at $0410 covering the six that can actually change: the carry loop in AddScore ends DEX/BNE, so X never reaches 0 and $040F is a permanent leading zero. Both labels are wanted -- $040F is the base the code indexes ($040F,X) and $0410 is the number -- but they had the same name for a while, which made $0413 render as scoreDigits+3 under one and scoreDigits+4 under the other with nothing to say which." },
        { "id": "cmt_adekhy", "address": "$1300", "text": "Snake segment flags, one byte per segment, 1-indexed like segmentColumn and segmentRow. Every read of this array in the whole cartridge, found by scanning for the three addressing forms the code uses (BD FF 12, BD 00 13, BD FE 12, 1D ...), is listed here - fourteen sites, and that is all of them:\n\n  $88FF AND #$40   is this the head\n  $892A AND #$02   which way is the head going\n  $894B ORA #$01   at the row-22 wrap\n  $8962 EOR #$03   bounce: flip bits 0 and 1 together\n  $8995 AND #$80   only erase a cell if this is set\n  $8A49 AND #$C0   head and tail together means a one-segment snake\n  $8A5F            copied, during the array shift-down\n  $8A82 ORA #$80   mark the segment before the removed one as the tail\n  $8A8D / $8A90    flags[X] |= flags[X-1], carrying the head bit past a deletion\n  $8AA7 AND #$40   walk backwards to find the head\n  $8AAE            loaded, then ORed into flags[X] at $8AC1 (the two NOPs between are padding, not a discard)\n  $8AB8 ORA #$80   the same tail marking on the other path\n\nBits in use: $80 tail, $40 head, $02 direction. And bit $01 is written twice - set at the wrap, flipped by the EOR #$03 at every bounce - and never once read. It is always the complement of bit 1, so it is one direction bit stored twice with only one copy consulted. Another piece of the same picture as the NOP runs at $834E and the 32-slot pod table used as 24.\n\nNothing here uses bits $04 to $20 at all." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "prg",
      "path": "gridrunner.prg",
      "id": "lay_owaapp",
      "regions": [
        { "id": "rgn_luq0pa", "start": "$8000", "end": "$800A", "kind": "data", "name": "CartridgeHeader", "comment": "C64 autostart cartridge header. $8000 = cold start vector ($83C1), $8002 = warm start / NMI vector ($83E2), $8004..$8008 = PETSCII \"CBM80\" (C3 C2 CD 38 30) which is what makes the KERNAL jump here at reset." },
        { "id": "rgn_ykrbmj", "start": "$800B", "end": "$8014", "kind": "code", "name": "OrphanCharsetCopyTail", "comment": "The surviving tail of an earlier copy of CopyCharacterSet that lived at $8000, before the nine-byte cartridge header was laid over its start. It reconstructs exactly:\n\n  8000  A2 00     LDX #$00        <- now C1 83, the cold start vector\n  8002  BD 00 8E  LDA $8E00,X     <- now E2 83 C3, the NMI vector and C\n  8005  9D 00 20  STA $2000,X     <- now C2 CD 38, BM8\n  8008  BD 00 8F  LDA $8F00,X     <- first two bytes now 30 00, the 0 of CBM80 and a pad\n  800B  9D 00 21  STA $2100,X     <- intact\n  800E  CA        DEX             <- intact\n  800F  D0 F1     BNE $8002       <- intact, and it lands exactly on the LDA above\n  8011  4C 00 81  JMP $8100       <- intact\n\nCompare $82EC-$82FF, which is the same routine byte for byte with the loop head at $82EE. The branch at $800F landing on $8002 is what makes this a reconstruction rather than a guess, and it is also the whole reason list_warnings reports the analysis arriving at $8000 and $8002." },
        { "id": "rgn_6g9bdi", "start": "$8014", "end": "$8015", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_8014" },
        { "id": "rgn_p2hs14", "start": "$801D", "end": "$8020", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_801D" },
        { "id": "rgn_x2fmds", "start": "$802B", "end": "$8030", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_802B" },
        { "id": "rgn_ef1frs", "start": "$803F", "end": "$8040", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_803F" },
        { "id": "rgn_e0qhmk", "start": "$805F", "end": "$8060", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_805F" },
        { "id": "rgn_8tfzo2", "start": "$8080", "end": "$80A0", "kind": "data", "name": "txtCopyrightPressFire", "comment": "32 characters in the game character set: (c) 1982 HES  PRESS FIRE TO BEGIN. Drawn at $0592 (row 10, column 2) in colour 7 by DrawCopyrightRow. The (c) is two half-glyphs, $3C and $3D." },
        { "id": "rgn_k304gs", "start": "$80FE", "end": "$8100", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_80FE" },
        { "id": "rgn_z456su", "start": "$8162", "end": "$8163", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_8162" },
        { "id": "rgn_zqfbtl", "start": "$837B", "end": "$8380", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_837B" },
        { "id": "rgn_vj8big", "start": "$839C", "end": "$83A0", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_839C" },
        { "id": "rgn_hscwb6", "start": "$83F0", "end": "$83FA", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_83F0" },
        { "id": "rgn_imwmek", "start": "$83FD", "end": "$8400", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_83FD" },
        { "id": "rgn_6t3l3a", "start": "$871F", "end": "$8728", "kind": "data", "name": "explosionFrameChain", "comment": "The explosion animation chain, indexed 1..7 by AdvanceExplosions. A cell holding entry N is rewritten as entry N+1 each pass: $18 sparks, $0D, $0E, $0F, $10, $11, $12 dispersing. Entry 7 ($12) is the end -- ExplosionBecomesPod turns that cell into a falling pod. Entry 0 ($EA) is never read; it is the NOP filler byte the search stops before." },
        { "id": "rgn_u8hsdm", "start": "$87D8", "end": "$87D9", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_87D8" },
        { "id": "rgn_9vpafu", "start": "$881F", "end": "$8847", "kind": "data", "name": "statusLineChars", "comment": "40 screen codes copied to $0400 by DrawStatusLine, one-indexed so $8820 is column 0. Reads: GRIDRUNNER, two spaces, PL, space, seven $30 score digits, two spaces, HI, space, seven $30 high-score digits, two spaces, char $07 (the ship, as the lives icon), two spaces, $34 = the digit 4. PL and HI are the small-font glyph pairs at $19/$1A and $1D/$1E; both match the big-font letter shapes stroke for stroke." },
        { "id": "rgn_ch26do", "start": "$8847", "end": "$886F", "kind": "data", "name": "statusLineColours", "comment": "The 40 colour-RAM bytes for the status row, again one-indexed at $8848. GRIDRUNNER is 3/4 (cyan into purple), the two score fields are 1 (white) with 7 (yellow) either side, the ship is $0D (light green) like the ship in play, and the lives digit is 4. Note $8847 is shared: it is both the last entry of statusLineChars and the base of this table." },
        { "id": "rgn_3r9vjc", "start": "$8984", "end": "$8990", "kind": "code", "name": "OrphanSnakeFragment" },
        { "id": "rgn_n52fw9", "start": "$8AD4", "end": "$8ADE", "kind": "code", "name": "OrphanPlotUnlessPlayer" },
        { "id": "rgn_36af5g", "start": "$8AE7", "end": "$8AE9", "kind": "code", "name": "pad_8AE7" },
        { "id": "rgn_qiqp36", "start": "$8AF1", "end": "$8AF8", "kind": "code", "name": "OrphanWaitForSpace" },
        { "id": "rgn_5t9f1i", "start": "$8B5D", "end": "$8B60", "kind": "code", "name": "OrphanDexLoop" },
        { "id": "rgn_g0v70l", "start": "$8BC0", "end": "$8BD2", "kind": "data", "name": "debrisDeltaTables", "comment": "Direction tables for the eight death fragments. debrisDeltaColumn is $8BC0 and debrisDeltaRow is $8BC8, both one-indexed, and they overlap at $8BC8 -- which is harmless because the shared byte is $80 in both readings. $80 means do not step, $00 means step once, $01 means step twice, and StepDebrisColumn/StepDebrisRow then subtract one, so the three cases are -1, 0 and +1 written the long way round. The eight pairs give the eight compass directions." },
        { "id": "rgn_v58trp", "start": "$8C51", "end": "$8C75", "kind": "text", "name": "LevelBannerStrings", "comment": "Two 18-character strings in the cartridge charset, drawn by the level-start code at $8C33 with X = $12 down to 1.\n\n$8C51 = \" BATTLE  STATIONS \" to $04FE-$050F in colour $0E, which is screen row 6 column 14.\n$8C63 = \"ENTER GRID AREA 00\" to $054E-$055F in colour $01, which is screen row 8 column 14.\n\n(An earlier version of this comment said rows 4 and 6; the arithmetic is $04FE-$0400 = 254 = 6*40+14 and $054E-$0400 = 334 = 8*40+14.)\n\nThe trailing \"00\" of the second string is not constant. It lands at $055E/$055F and the loop at $8C9D counts it up to the level number one INC at a time, carrying by hand, and recolours those two cells through the whole palette during the fanfare at $8D16.", "view": "snippet:dec_i24r3l" },
        { "id": "rgn_rrrzi9", "start": "$8CB5", "end": "$8D16", "kind": "data", "name": "LevelDifficultyTables", "comment": "Three 1-indexed per-level tables, read at $8C8C with X = levelNumber ($01-$1F): $8CB4 waves, $8CD4 segments per wave, $8CF4 zapper interval." },
        { "id": "rgn_n344n1", "start": "$8DCB", "end": "$8DE1", "kind": "data", "name": "TitleTailPadding", "comment": "$8DCB-$8DCC is B0 8D, which reads as the address $8DB0 - TitleJoystickLoop, the routine 27 bytes above. It is the operand of a JMP whose $4C opcode is not there: the instruction before it, at $8DC8, is a complete JMP $80E5. So this is two bytes of a removed instruction, not data anybody reads. $8DCD-$8DE0 is twenty $EA bytes padding out to the strings at $8DE1, the longest NOP run in the cartridge." },
        { "id": "rgn_cw8074", "start": "$8DE1", "end": "$8E00", "kind": "text", "name": "ScreenStrings", "comment": "Two 15-character strings in the cartridge charset: $8DE1 \"BY JEFF MINTER \" and $8DF1 \"ENTER LEVEL 00 \", with an $EA at $8DF0 between them (shown as ~ by the decoder). DrawTitleScreenText copies only 14 characters of each, X = $0E down to 1, so the trailing space never reaches the screen - which is what puts the two digits of the string at $0557/$0558, exactly where PrintTitleLevelNumber writes the chosen level.", "view": "snippet:dec_i24r3l" },
        { "id": "rgn_z06cib", "start": "$8E00", "end": "$9000", "kind": "data", "name": "CharacterSet", "comment": "The cartridge character set, 64 glyphs of 8 bytes, code C at $8E00 + 8*C. Copied to $2000 by CopyCharacterSet; $D018 = $18 points the VIC there. The full glyph-by-glyph inventory, each tied to the instruction that uses it, is in the comment on this address below. (An earlier version of this region comment guessed several glyphs from their shapes and got $0A and $13-$1A wrong; it has been replaced.)", "view": "char:8" }
      ],
