      ],
      "comments": [
        { "id": "cmt_rp6s5h", "address": "$8100", "text": "Video and screen-geometry setup.\n$D018=$18 puts the screen at $0400 and the character set at $2000; border\nand background both go black. SID voices 1 and 2 get their envelopes.\nThen it builds two 24-entry tables of screen row addresses at\nscreenRowLoTable/screenRowHiTable ($0400 + 40*row) and, in the same loop,\nfills every row with $20 -- so the tables are built and the screen cleared\nin one pass." },
        { "id": "cmt_j9lxkp", "address": "$8163", "text": "Screen address for the current row: reads screenRowLoTable/HiTable at\nindex plotRow into screenPtrLo/Hi, and loads Y with plotColumn.\nSo plotRow indexes the table and plotColumn is the Y offset." },
        { "id": "cmt_o39j5l", "address": "$8172", "text": "Plot one cell. Writes plotChar to the screen at (plotColumn, plotRow),\nthen adds $D4 to screenPtrHi -- $04xx + $D4 = $D8xx -- and writes\nplotColour to colour RAM at the same cell. The three LDA $07 in a row at\n$8180-$8184 do nothing; they are padding." },
        { "id": "cmt_5x04qv", "address": "$818B", "text": "The read half of PlotCharAndColour: returns in A the screen code already\nat (plotColumn, plotRow). This is how the game does collision detection --\nit looks at the screen rather than at a model of it." },
        { "id": "cmt_svkwk3", "address": "$8191", "text": "The whole sound engine in one habit. All 66 SID writes in the binary go to\nfrequency-high ($D401/$D408/$D40F), control, envelope and master volume.\nThe frequency-low registers, the pulse width and the entire filter section\nare never written -- so every pitch is one byte and every effect is a\nsweep of that byte. Control values used: $00 gate off, $81 noise, $11\ntriangle, $21 sawtooth, $25 sawtooth with ring modulation (level fanfare)." },
        { "id": "cmt_lwcx5h", "address": "$81A2", "text": "The grid wipe, and it is also what defines the walls.\n\nTwo passes over the play area, each cell plotted through PlotCharAndColour\nwith a noise blip and a rising-pitch delay per column, so it is an\nanimation rather than a fill: pass one lays char $3F (a solid bar) in\norange down every column from 2 to 38, rows 2 to 21; pass two lays char\n$00 (the grid crosshair) across columns 1 to 38 of the same rows, erasing\nit again and leaving the playfield.\n\nWhat that leaves behind is the boundary system for the whole game. Inside\nthe box the cells are $00; everything outside it is still the $20 that\nInitVideoAndScreenTables wrote. Every collision test in the game is BEQ\nafter ReadScreenChar, which means \"is this cell $00\", so a space reads as\noccupied and acts as a wall. The snake bounces off columns 0 and 39 for\nexactly that reason, and MoveOnePodDown uses the same fact to know a pod\nhas left the grid. There is no separate boundary check anywhere." },
        { "id": "cmt_pcivg1", "address": "$821B", "text": "Pause, and a correction to what I first wrote here: this is a tail call,\nnot an abandoned return.\n\n$C5 is the KERNAL current-key byte -- which works only because the stock\ntimer IRQ is still running -- and $29 is matrix code 41, P. Not P: return\nat once. P: spin until it is released, spin until it is pressed again,\nthen JMP WaitPauseKeyRelease, which spins until that second press is\nreleased and RTSs. That RTS returns to MainLoop, so the JMP is just a\nshared tail. The pause itself is those spin loops: the entire game stops\ninside them, because there is no interrupt doing anything." },
        { "id": "cmt_h4w2ho", "address": "$8230", "text": "Delay with a rising pitch: counts $0A from 5 round to 4 (255 passes),\nwriting the current column ($08) to V1 frequency high and column+1 to V2,\nso the wipe sound sweeps as the wipe crosses the screen." },
        { "id": "cmt_u8to3f", "address": "$82EC", "text": "Copies the cartridge character set to $2000-$21FF: $8E00->$2000 and\n$8F00->$2100, 256 bytes each in one loop. 512 bytes is 64 characters, so\nevery screen code this game uses is $00-$3F." },
        { "id": "cmt_m31q4q", "address": "$8373", "text": "Reads $DC11, which is the CIA1 mirror of $DC01 -- port B, joystick 1 --\nand stores the inverted value in joystickState, so a set bit means a\ndirection or the fire button is active." },
        { "id": "cmt_2ck2c2", "address": "$8393", "text": "Top of the main loop. It is a JMP chain, not a JSR/RTS structure:\n$8393 -> $83A3 (the nine per-frame calls) -> $83E8 (a short DEX delay)\n-> $83A0 -> $8393. Each pass reads the joystick and checks for pause." },
        { "id": "cmt_dl1gun", "address": "$83C1", "text": "Cold start, reached through the cartridge vector at $8000.\nSEI/CLD, then $D016=$05 (38-column mode, X-scroll 5), the four KERNAL\ninitialisation calls IOINIT/RAMTAS/RESTOR/CINT, CLI, and CHR$(8) through\nCHROUT which locks out the Commodore-Shift character-set switch.\nFalls through to the game via JMP $82EC." },
        { "id": "cmt_8es5tp", "address": "$83E2", "text": "NMI handler, reached through the cartridge vector at $8002.\nThe KERNAL NMI path pushes A, X and Y before jumping here; this pops all\nthree back and RTIs, so RESTORE does nothing. Stack delta is -3 (RTI), not\n-2: this is an interrupt return, not a subroutine return." },
        { "id": "cmt_ehaxgp", "address": "$871F", "text": "1-indexed: entry 0 is $EA filler" },
        { "id": "cmt_v3n2ju", "address": "$8870", "text": "Add to the score, which is stored nowhere but the screen. The seven digits\nlive at $040F-$0415 in screen RAM as characters $30-$39, and this walks\nthe carry leftwards: increment digit X, and if it passes $39 set it to\n$30 and carry into X-1. Y is how many times to add. X selects the column,\nso X=6 adds ones and X=4 adds hundreds." },
        { "id": "cmt_k7czoz", "address": "$888A", "text": "Score 10 (X=6 units digit, Y=$0A) and then arm the falling sound sweep: sweepFreqHi = $F0, sweepRepeats = 3." },
        { "id": "cmt_o5pei7", "address": "$889A", "text": "The falling sweep on SID voice 3. Runs only while zappersEnabled bit 0 is set. Walks sweepFreqHi down from $F0 into $D40F, re-gating $D412 to $00 then $21 (sawtooth + gate) on every step so each step retriggers; when the top two bits of the frequency have gone, reload $F0 and count one off sweepRepeats. With repeats exhausted it parks $D40F at $04 and stops. Three descending swoops." },
        { "id": "cmt_os9de6", "address": "$88C9", "text": "The snake: a chain of segments walking down the grid, held in three\nparallel arrays indexed 1..segmentCount -- segmentColumn ($1100),\nsegmentRow ($1200) and segmentFlags ($1300), all addressed one-indexed as\nbase-1,X.\n\nIt is walked from the tail upward. A segment without flag bit 6 simply\ncopies the position of the segment in front of it, which is what makes the\nbody follow the head. The one with bit 6 is the head: it steps one column\nleft or right depending on flag bit 1, and when the cell it wants is not\nempty it reverses (SnakeStepIntoCell), drops a row, and flips the\ndirection bits. Reaching row 22 wraps it back to row 14, column 2.\n\nFlag bit 7 means the segment is on screen and must be erased before it is\nredrawn; a freshly spawned one does not have it yet." },
        { "id": "cmt_wxribr", "address": "$89C1", "text": "Spawn. New segments enter at column 10, row 2, one every spawnCountdown\npasses. segmentsLeftInWave counts a wave down; the first of a wave gets\nflags $41 (head, direction), the rest $00, and the last gets $80.\nWhen a wave ends, wavesLeftThisLevel is decremented. Both counts come from\nthe per-level tables at $8CD4 and $8CB4." },
        { "id": "cmt_mvea8j", "address": "$8A11", "text": "The laser hit something that is not an explosion, so it is a snake\nsegment. Char $13 scores 100; $14 or $15 score 400, and those two are only\never shown by the head, whose char cycles $13-$15 -- so the head is worth\nfour times a body segment two passes in three and the same as one on the\nthird. Whether that is intended or an oversight cannot be told from here.\n\nIt then finds the segment whose column and row match the hit cell, removes\nit by shifting every higher-numbered entry down one, and leaves char $0F\nbehind so the explosion engine takes over. Killing the head promotes the\nnext segment by OR-ing the flags along.\n\nPLA PLA before the search: this abandons UpdatePlayerLaser entirely and\nreturns to the main loop, so the rest of the laser update is skipped for\nthis pass." },
        { "id": "cmt_qdmf79", "address": "$8ADE", "text": "Player killed. LDX #$F6 / TXS throws the whole call stack away rather\nthan unwinding, which is why five different places can jump straight here\nfrom any depth. stackDelta is undefined at this instruction for exactly\nthat reason." },
        { "id": "cmt_couwje", "address": "$8AF8", "text": "Death animation: eight fragments thrown out from the player position.\ndebrisColumn/debrisRow ($1500/$1600) hold their positions, all eight\nstarting at the ship; debrisDeltaColumn/debrisDeltaRow at $8BC0/$8BC8 give\neach one a direction as $80 (none), $00 (one step) or $01 (two steps),\nfrom which StepDebrisColumn/Row subtract one -- so the encoding is really\n-1, 0, +1 written the long way round. The two tables overlap by a byte at\n$8BC8, which is legitimate: it is $80 in both.\n\nThe fragments cycle through chars $16-$18, run for 15 outer passes, and\nare then cleared by ClearPlayArea." },
        { "id": "cmt_9uo989", "address": "$8BEC", "text": "The lethality rule, and it is the keystone of the whole design.\n\nNothing that moves checks whether the player is in the way before drawing\nitself; the zapper beam, the snake body and the laser all just write their\ncharacter into a cell. What catches that is MovePlayer, which every time\nit runs reads the cell the ship is supposed to be in and, if the ship is\nnot there any more, asks this routine what replaced it. $08 and $09 (the\nplayer laser passing through) and $00 (the empty grid) are survivable;\neverything else is death.\n\nSo 'did something hit me' is answered by 'is my own character still on the\nscreen where I left it', one frame late, and the beam chars $05/$06 and\nthe snake char $13 kill through this path rather than through a collision\ntest of their own. Only three things test for the ship directly --\nCheckPlayerAtCell, SnakeHeadChecksCell and MoveOnePodDown -- and those are\nthe cases where the object stops there and must kill on the step." },
        { "id": "cmt_vrsbsp", "address": "$8BFC", "text": "Clears the playfield to spaces, $0450 to $07FF - screen rows 2 down, leaving the status line and the score alone.\n\nWorth noting for anyone reading zero page: the pointer here is ($07),$08, so $07 is a pointer LOW byte and $08 the high byte. $07 is named screenPtrHi from its use in ScreenAddrForRow, where the pair is $06/$07. The two routines use overlapping but differently aligned pointers in the same zero-page bytes." },
        { "id": "cmt_ve0bk9", "address": "$8C2D", "text": "Start a level. Resets the stack pointer to $F6 outright rather than returning to anyone - this is entered by JMP from three places and abandons whatever call chain led here.\n\nDraws the two banner strings, then unconditionally INCs livesDigit (capped at $39) and levelNumber (capped at $1F), loads the three per-level difficulty values, and counts the two-digit banner at $055E/$055F up to the level number one INC at a time.\n\nThat unconditional pair of INCs is why the death path decrements twice: $8C1D takes a life, then $8D5E takes another and also backs levelNumber off by one, so that arriving here nets out as \"one life fewer, same level\". Neither decrement is a bug; read either on its own and it looks like one." },
        { "id": "cmt_37fihl", "address": "$8CB4", "text": "Per-level difficulty, three tables read together at $8C8C with X = levelNumber. All three are 1-indexed, so the byte at the label itself is never read. $8CB4 = waves of snake, $8CD4 = segments in a wave, $8CF4 = zapper interval (smaller is faster).\n\nDecoded in full, with the product because that is what the level actually costs you:\n\n  lvl waves segs zap   total     lvl waves segs zap   total\n   1    1     6   16     6        17    6    17    8    102\n   2    2     6   16    12        18    6    18    8    108\n   3    2     6   16    12        19    6    19    8    114\n   4    3     7   15    21        20    6    20    8    120\n   5    3     7   14    21        21    6    20    7    120\n   6    3     8   13    24        22    6    20    7    120\n   7    4     8   12    32        23    6    21    7    126\n   8    4     9   11    36        24    6    21    7    126\n   9    4    12   10    48        25    6    22    7    132\n  10    4    12    9    48        26    6    22    7    132\n  11    5    10    9    50        27    7    22    7    154\n  12    5    10    9    50        28    7    23    7    161\n  13   16     3    9    48        29    7     3    7     21\n  14    6    15    9    90        30    7    24    6    168\n  15    6    16    9    96        31    7    24    6    168\n  16    6    16    9    96\n\nThe zapper interval is the clean one: $10 at level 1 falling to $06 at level 31, monotonic apart from plateaus. Segments per wave climbs monotonically too, 6 to 24.\n\nTwo levels break the pattern and they break it differently, which is why they are worth separating:\n\n  Level 13 is 16 waves of 3 rather than 5 waves of 10. The total, 48, is exactly what levels 9 and 10 gave you - so this is not a spike, it is the same amount of snake delivered as sixteen short ones. A change of shape.\n\n  Level 29 is 7 waves of 3 = 21 segments, sitting between 161 and 168. That is an outright breather - less snake than level 4 - twenty-eight levels in.\n\nThey are 16 apart, and both are made by dropping segments-per-wave to 3 while leaving waves alone. Whether that is a designed rest beat or two hand-edited bytes I cannot say from the binary; what is certain is that both are single-byte deviations in $8CD4 and neither is a decode artefact, since the neighbouring entries are ordinary.\n\nLevel rolls over at $20 in StartLevel, so 31 is the last distinct row and the table has no 32nd entry: $8CD4+$20 is $8CF4, the first table's unused byte, which is $19." },
        { "id": "cmt_p4orku", "address": "$8060", "text": "Game over: compare the seven score digits at $040F with the seven high\nscore digits at $041B, most significant first. Equal digits keep looking,\na lower one gives up, a higher one copies the whole score across. Both\nnumbers are read straight out of screen RAM." },
        { "id": "cmt_ztmy9f", "address": "$824F", "text": "Materialise the ship. animRadius counts from $0F down to zero; each pass\nplots debris chars $16-$18 at five points offset from (20,21) by that\nradius, waits, erases them, and shrinks. When the radius underflows the\nship is drawn at (20,21) and three descending sweeps play." },
        { "id": "cmt_feyxor", "address": "$8470", "text": "Move the player. Gated on masterTick reaching 0, so about once every 129\npasses of the main loop.\n\nErases the old cell, applies the four joystick directions from\njoystickState with hard clamps -- rows 15..21, columns 1..38, so the ship\nis confined to the bottom third of the grid -- then looks at the\ndestination cell before committing. Not empty means either an explosion,\nwhich IsExplosionChar turns into \"stay where you were\", or something\nCheckDebrisCellSafe treats as fatal. Finally redraws the ship, char $07\nin colour $0D." },
        { "id": "cmt_orv5r7", "address": "$84F8", "text": "The player laser. Fires on joystick bit 4 and travels straight up.\n\nlaserRow = $FF means no laser. On fire it starts at the player column,\none row above the player, with laserCharPhase $08 and laserSoundCounter\n$40. Each tick it looks at the cell ahead: anything that is neither the\nlaser char itself nor empty is a hit and goes to LaserHitExplosion. The\nphase counts $08 to $09 and only then does the row decrement, so the bolt\nis drawn as two sub-cell frames per row travelled. It expires at row 2." },
        { "id": "cmt_du8yik", "address": "$859B", "text": "The two edge zappers. The X zapper is char $01 walking down column 0,\nrows 3..21; the Y zapper is char $02 walking right along row 22, columns\n1..38. Both step every second pass on which masterTick is 1.\n\nzapCountdown counts down to the moment they fire: it reloads from\nzapIntervalForLevel, triggers a sawtooth on voice 3, and arms the beam by\nsetting beamActive $FF and latching the two zapper positions into\nbeamRow/beamFiredFromColumn." },
        { "id": "cmt_xw33t9", "address": "$8635", "text": "Draw the zapper beam once it is armed. Runs only on the pass where\nlaserTickCounter is 5, so it is deliberately out of phase with the laser.\n\nThe Y zapper fires a vertical bar up its own column, rows 21 down to 3,\nin the animating char beamCharPhase; the X zapper walks a horizontal head\nrightwards from column beamColumn along beamRow, one cell per pass,\nchecking the cell it enters. When the two meet -- the cell already holds\nbeamCharPhase -- the vertical bar is erased and the meeting point is left\nas char $0F in yellow, which is an explosion frame, so AdvanceExplosions\ntakes it from there." },
        { "id": "cmt_tjnz7f", "address": "$86D7", "text": "The explosion engine, and it is a whole-screen scan rather than a list.\nEvery pass on which zapCountdown is 5, it walks $0450-$07FF looking at\nevery cell; anything matching an entry of explosionFrameChain is rewritten\nas the next entry along. Reaching the last entry ($12) calls\nExplosionBecomesPod.\n\nThis is why there is no explosion table anywhere: the screen is the model.\nThe same trick is used for collision -- ReadScreenChar rather than a\nposition list -- throughout the game." },
        { "id": "cmt_3vw35p", "address": "$8728", "text": "End of the explosion: the cell becomes a pod (char $0A) and is registered\nin the 24-slot table podScreenPtrLo/Hi, which stores the screen address\nrather than a column and row. If every slot is taken the cell is put back\nto $12 and stays in the last explosion frame until one frees up -- so the\npod limit shows up as explosions that will not finish." },
        { "id": "cmt_i0mcr6", "address": "$8753", "text": "Pods fall. Every $40 passes, each occupied slot has 40 added to its stored\nscreen address, which is exactly one row down.\n\nMoveOnePodDown reuses plotColumn/plotRow ($02/$03) as the pointer rather\nthan as a column and row -- the same two bytes, a different meaning, and\nthe reason the listing shows STA (plotColumn),Y. Landing on char $07 is\nthe player and jumps to PlayerKilled; landing on $20 means it has left the\ngrid and the slot is freed." },
        { "id": "cmt_g2karj", "address": "$8806", "text": "Draw the status row, 40 characters and 40 colours copied to $0400 and\n$D800 from the tables at $8820 and $8848, one-indexed. It reads\n\"GRIDRUNNER\" followed by a two-character glyph pair at $040D-$040E, the\nseven-digit score at $040F-$0415, the seven-digit high score at\n$041B-$0421, and the lives digit at $0427." },
        { "id": "cmt_titua3", "address": "$8AC8", "text": "Level complete: no waves left to spawn and no segments alive. Note the\nzappers and any falling pods do not have to be dealt with -- only the\nsnake counts." },
        { "id": "cmt_jvwzfs", "address": "$8820", "text": "The status row as literal screen data: 40 characters at $8820 and 40 colours at $8848, both 1-indexed, copied straight to $0400 and $D800.\n\nDecoded through the cartridge charset it is: $0400-$0409 \"GRIDRUNNER\", with $0400-$0403 in colour 3 (cyan) and $0404-$0409 in colour 4 (purple) - the two-tone logo. $040C/$040D and $0418/$0419 are two-glyph marks from the graphics half of the charset in colour 7, labelling the two number fields; I could not read them as letters and am not going to guess. $040F-$0415 is a seven-digit score field in cyan, $041B-$0421 a seven-digit high score in light blue, $0424 is $07 - the player ship glyph - in light green, and $0427 is the lives digit, initialised here to $34 = \"4\"." },
        { "id": "cmt_wwlh9d", "address": "$8870", "placement": "inline", "text": "seven digits are drawn, only six can ever change - see the note below" },
        { "id": "cmt_0abkep", "address": "$8881", "placement": "inline", "text": "X reaches 0 here and the loop stops, so the carry never reaches $040F" },
        { "id": "cmt_3xryca", "address": "$800A", "text": "$800A is the high byte of the LDA $8F00,X operand above, so the code stream resumes at $800B, not here." },
        { "id": "cmt_6v2zqw", "address": "$83FA", "text": "Dead: a JMP MainLoop sitting in the middle of ten NOPs, reached by nothing. The live path into the loop is the JMP at $83F0-adjacent $83ED and the one at $8370." },
        { "id": "cmt_e21v6d", "address": "$8984", "text": "Dead. LDX #$01 / INC snakeHeadChar / RTS, then a second stub LDX savedIndex / INX / CPX segmentCount / RTS at $8989. Nothing reaches either. The second one walks the segment array *upward* where UpdateSnake walks it downward with DEX, so this looks like the remains of an earlier version of the snake loop rather than a routine that lost its caller." },
        { "id": "cmt_94h0ix", "address": "$8AD4", "text": "Dead. JSR ReadScreenChar / CMP #$07 / BEQ PlayerHit / JMP PlotCharAndColour -- \"plot here unless the player is standing on it\". Nothing calls it. It reads like a safer plot primitive that was written and then not used; the game does this check inline in three other places instead." },
        { "id": "cmt_axqsfs", "address": "$8AF1", "text": "Dead. CMP #$20 / BEQ back one byte / JMP PlayerHit. The branch target is $8AF0, the RTS at the end of SnakeHeadChecksCell, so as written it is not even self-consistent -- more evidence this is a fragment left behind rather than a routine." },
        { "id": "cmt_y40xbj", "address": "$8D16", "text": "Level-start fanfare, and the only place all three SID voices are driven together.\n\nfanfareCounter ($36) runs $30 down to 1. Each outer pass writes the counter itself into colour RAM at $D95E/$D95F - the two level-number digits of the \"ENTER GRID AREA nn\" banner - so those digits cycle through all sixteen colours as the sound plays. The inner loop runs fanfareCounter times, so the passes get shorter as the pitch falls and the whole thing accelerates.\n\nEach inner step: a delay, then SetVoice1PitchFromX puts $40 - X into $D401 and the same value is stored into $D408 and $D40F, so all three voices track one pitch; then all three control registers are gated off ($00) and on again with $25 = sawtooth + ring modulation + gate. Ring modulation on all three at the same frequency is what gives it the metallic edge.\n\nEnds by jumping to InitLevelVariables, so it is the last thing before play starts." },
        { "id": "cmt_g6u5y0", "address": "$8D8E", "text": "Draws the title screen text. Two 14-character copies, both 1-indexed from X = $0E: TxtByJeffMinter to $04FB-$0508 in colour 3, TxtEnterLevel to $054B-$0558 in colour 1 - screen rows 6 and 8, both starting at column 11. Sets levelNumber to 1 on the way in, then falls through to DrawCopyrightRow.\n\nOnly 14 of the 15 characters of each string are copied: the loop runs X = $0E down to 1 and the strings are 15 bytes, so the trailing space is never written. Harmless because the screen was cleared, and it is why the last two characters of \"ENTER LEVEL 00\" land on $0557/$0558, which is exactly where PrintTitleLevelNumber writes the digits." },
        { "id": "cmt_47ikjf", "address": "$8DB0", "text": "The title screen input loop, and the whole of the front end's interaction.\n\nIt polls $DC11, which is the CIA1 mirror of $DC01 - joystick port 1. $EF is %11101111, bit 4 low, which is fire: jump to TitleFireStartsGame. $FE is %11111110, bit 0 low, which is up: increment levelNumber and redraw the two digits at $0557/$0558, then keep polling. Any other value spins.\n\nSo the player chooses a starting level by pushing up and starts with fire; there is nothing else on the title screen. The level rolls over at $20 back to 1, in PrintTitleLevelNumber rather than here." },
        { "id": "cmt_z5ujxq", "address": "$8062", "placement": "inline", "text": "digit-by-digit from the most significant end: a string compare, valid because both fields are fixed width and zero padded" },
        { "id": "cmt_i8ac7w", "address": "$837B", "text": "Padding. Runs of $EA and a few stray $60 bytes sit between routines all\nthrough the cartridge: $8014, $801D, $802B, $803F, $805F, $80FE, $8162,\n$837B, $839C, $83F0, $83FD, $87D8, $881E and $8AE7, about sixty bytes in\nall. I have declared each as code rather than data, because $EA is a NOP\nand execution passing through one is legitimate, where declaring it data\nwould be a claim that execution stops -- which nothing here knows.\n\nOne consequence to be aware of: a pad declared code immediately before a\ndata table makes the walk fall through into the table and raises a\nwarning. That happened at $881E, in front of statusLineChars, so that one\nis deliberately left undeclared." },
        { "id": "cmt_q5v5xk", "address": "$83D4", "text": "The only CLI, matched by the only real SEI three instructions earlier --\n$8AE5 is a false positive, it is the $78 inside JMP $8D78. Nothing is\never written to $D019/$D01A or $0314-$0319, so the game installs no\ninterrupt of its own and leaves the stock KERNAL timer IRQ running. That\nis what keeps $C5 updated for the pause key, and it is also why nothing\nhere is synchronised to the raster." },
        { "id": "cmt_m15unc", "address": "$8652", "placement": "inline", "text": "beamCharPhase alternates $05/$06 and resets at $07: those two glyphs" },
        { "id": "cmt_d370q9", "address": "$8698", "placement": "inline", "text": "CLC then SBC subtracts 2, so the trailing head is glyph $03 or $04" },
        { "id": "cmt_93og72", "address": "$8E58", "text": "Glyphs $0B and $0C are a matched pair of left- and right-pointing beam\nheads. Nothing loads either value and no animation chain can produce\nthem -- the beam uses $03-$06 instead -- so this is unused art. $1C (a\nsecond copy of the Y at $1B) and $1F are unused for the same reason.\n$1B is Y, used by \"BY JEFF MINTER\"; $3B is V, used by \"ENTER LEVEL\";\nthose two are easy to swap by eye and only the strings settle it." },
        { "id": "cmt_7nbcbz", "address": "$8E00", "text": "The game's own 64-glyph 8x8 character set. Copied verbatim to $2000-$21FF by CopyCharacterSet, and $D018 = $18 points the VIC at $2000, so the screen code C is the glyph at $8E00 + 8*C and every code this program uses is $00-$3F.\n\nThat is why neither built-in text reading works on this cartridge: the strings are in this encoding, not PETSCII and not screen codes.\n\nGraphics half, $00-$1F. Every identification below is from a use site, not from the picture:\n  $00 grid cell, a cross. What the play area is paved with, and what erasing anything paints back.\n  $01 X zapper, $02 Y zapper. From MoveZappers at $85CD/$85F1.\n  $03 $04 X-zapper beam head, two frames. $05 $06 Y-zapper beam, two frames. UpdateZapperBeam INCs beamCharPhase and resets it at $07 to $05, so the vertical bar alternates $05/$06; the horizontal head is drawn at $869B with CLC then SBC #$01, which subtracts TWO, not one - so it is $03 and $04. That CLC is the only reason those two glyphs are ever reachable.\n  $07 the player ship. Three separate collision tests compare against it ($8023, $8AD9, $8AEB) and it is also the life icon at $0424.\n  $08 $09 the player laser bolt, low and high in the cell. laserCharPhase runs $08, $09, then resets to $08 and only then decrements the row, so the bolt moves half a cell at a time and the two glyphs are vertical mirrors of each other for that reason.\n  $0A pod. Written by ExplosionBecomesPod.\n  $0B $0C left- and right-pointing horizontal bolts. UNUSED: no instruction anywhere in the cartridge loads $0B or $0C as an immediate (find_immediates returns nothing for either) and no data table contains them. They are drawn and never reached.\n  $0D $0E $0F $10 $11 $12 explosion frames 2-7, in that order, from explosionFrameChain at $871F. The chain starts at $18 and ends at $12, which becomes a pod.\n  $13 $14 $15 snake body, three frames. snakeHeadChar cycles them at $88DE.\n  $16 $17 $18 flying debris in the player death animation, cycled at $8B64. $18 is doing double duty: it is also frame 1 of the explosion chain. A good demonstration of why a value gets a name per use site rather than a global one.\n  $19 $1A and $1D $1E two glyph pairs that appear only in statusLineChars, at $040C/$040D before the score and $0418/$0419 before the high score, both in yellow. They are field labels of some kind and I could not read them as letters. Their bitmaps, so somebody else can try:\n      $19 ........  $1A ........   $1D ........  $1E ........\n          ####..##       ......##       ##..##..      ####....\n          ##.##.##       ......##       ##..##..      .##..##.\n          ####..##       ......##       ######..      .##.....\n          ##....##       ......##       ##..##..      .##..##.\n          ##....##       ##.##.##       ##..##..      ####....\n          ........       ........       ........      ........\n          ........       ........       ........      ........\n  $1B $1C the letter Y - two byte-identical glyphs at $8ED8 and $8EE0. Only $1B is used, in \"BY JEFF MINTER\".\n  $1F a right-pointing arrow. Not used by any instruction either.\n\nText half, $20-$3F:\n  $20 space\n  $21-$2F  G R U I D N E A B F H J M P S\n  $30      the letter O, which also serves as the digit 0\n  $31-$39  the digits 1 to 9\n  $3A T  $3B V  $3C $3D a two-glyph (c) mark  $3E L  $3F a full-height vertical bar, used by DrawGridWipe\nLetters are in ad-hoc order - roughly the order the game needed them - but the digits are deliberately at $30-$39 exactly as in ASCII, because AddScore does its carry arithmetic directly on the character codes.\nThere is no C, K, Q, W, X or Z in this font, and no lower case. The whole game says only: GRIDRUNNER, BY JEFF MINTER, ENTER LEVEL, (c) 1982 HES, PRESS FIRE TO BEGIN, BATTLE STATIONS, ENTER GRID AREA." },
        { "id": "cmt_2k1dlc", "address": "$8652", "text": "The vertical bar and the horizontal head are drawn with different characters, two apart. beamCharPhase alternates $05/$06 (reset at $07 by the test above); the head at $869B is beamCharPhase minus two because the SBC there is preceded by CLC rather than SEC. So the four beam glyphs $03 $04 $05 $06 are all reachable, and $03/$04 are reachable only through that carry." },
        { "id": "cmt_3kx9mm", "address": "$869B", "placement": "inline", "text": "CLC not SEC, so this subtracts TWO: phase $05 gives $03, $06 gives $04" },
        { "id": "cmt_iqgpoa", "address": "$8172", "placement": "inline", "text": "$04xx + $D4 never carries, so this always returns with C clear - relied on at $829B" },
        { "id": "cmt_l7ojzv", "address": "$824F", "placement": "inline", "text": "five arms, four with CLC/SBC, one without - see the note at $829B" },
        { "id": "cmt_rrtrpf", "address": "$829B", "placement": "inline", "text": "no CLC here, unlike the other four - safe only because PlotCharAndColour always returns C=0" },
        { "id": "cmt_js3xgw", "address": "$8748", "text": "Clears the pod table by writing $FF to every high byte, which is the empty marker.\n\nIt clears 32 slots (LDX #$20, so podScreenPtrHi-1+1 .. +32 = $1020-$103F) but the two routines that use the table only ever scan 24: ExplosionBecomesPod at $872C and MovePods at $875C both start LDX #$18. Slots 25 to 32 are therefore initialised and never looked at, in either direction - a pod cannot be placed there and one placed there could not fall.\n\nSo the real pod limit is 24, and the table is sized for 32. Same shape as the NOP runs and the self-assignments noted at $834E: a number that was changed in one place and not the others." },
        { "id": "cmt_89oupv", "address": "$876C", "text": "Verified by execution rather than by reading: with slot 1 holding $0550\nand Y=0, this writes $00 to $0550, $08 to $D950 and leaves the slot at\n$0578 -- $0550 plus $28, exactly one screen row down." },
        { "id": "cmt_v7jsfy", "address": "$87CB", "text": "The laser hit something. This routine handles only the explosion chain;\nanything else falls through to LaserHitSegment.\n\nWhat it does is REWIND the explosion one frame, not finish it: hitting a\ncell holding chain entry N rewrites it as entry N-1 in yellow. Since every\nexplosion in play starts at entry 4 ($0F) -- RemoveSnakeSegment and\nUpdateZapperBeam are the only things that start one, and both write $0F --\nfour hits walk a cell back $0F, $0E, $0D, $18, and the fourth (entry 1)\nclears the cell outright and scores 10 through AwardPodPointsAndSound.\nSo an explosion can be shot out before it turns into a pod, at one hit per\nshot because every branch sets laserRow to $FF.\n\nThat also explains why chain entry 1 exists: nothing ever writes $18 to\nthe screen during play -- find_immediates $18 and the list of writers of\nplotChar between them show it is only reachable by rewinding.\n\nBoth exits do PLA PLA, abandoning UpdatePlayerLaser and returning straight\nto the main loop." },
        { "id": "cmt_h4dtwp", "address": "$8A1D", "text": "Three NOPs where a fourth case was. LaserHitSegment tests $13, $14 and\n$15 and then falls off the end, so a laser hitting a POD (char $0A) does\nnothing at all -- LaserHitExplosion does not recognise $0A either, and\nreturns here, and here returns to UpdatePlayerLaser, which then plots the\nlaser straight over the pod. The pod stays in the table and MoveOnePodDown\nrewrites its character on the next move. Pods are unshootable, and the\nthree dead bytes are where that case used to be or was going to be." },
        { "id": "cmt_2vv0gt", "address": "$8A21", "text": "Scoring: X is the digit column and Y the number of increments, and column\n4 of $040F-$0415 is the hundreds. So a segment showing $14 or $15 scores\n300 here and 100 immediately below, a total of 400, while one showing $13\nfalls straight into the 100. $13 is what every body segment is drawn as\nand also one of the three characters the head cycles through -- so the\nhead is worth 400 on two passes in three and 100 on the third. Whether\nthat is the intended reward for hitting the head or an accident of\nreusing snakeHeadChar as the test cannot be settled from the code." },
        { "id": "cmt_am3nsg", "address": "$8163", "placement": "inline", "text": "Verified by execution: with plotRow 5 and the row tables holding $04C8 for row 5, screenPtr comes out $04C8, and PlotCharAndColour then writes the character at $04D4 for column 12 and the colour at $D8D4." },
        { "id": "cmt_m2fmhj", "address": "$8300", "text": "Level start, entered from the fanfare at $8D70 and leaving through JMP\n$83A0 into the main loop.\n\nIt sets the SID up, draws the grid (DrawGridWipe) and materialises the\nship (MaterialisePlayerShip), then plants the starting state: player at\ncolumn $14 row $15, laser off, X zapper at row 2, Y zapper at column 1,\nzapper move countdown 4, zap countdown $0A, and clears the pod table.\nwavesLeftThisLevel, segmentsPerWave and zapIntervalForLevel are NOT set\nhere -- they come from the three per-level tables and this routine only\ntouches them with the no-op sequence at $834E." },
        { "id": "cmt_vwbd8n", "address": "$834E", "text": "Six instructions that do nothing: LDA $2B / STA $2B / STA $2B and LDA $2A / STA $2A. Both values were already set from the per-level tables at $8C8C and are being preserved rather than initialised -- this is where something used to be." },
        { "id": "cmt_7r6j7h", "address": "$892A", "text": "The head. Flag bit 1 clear means step right (INC INC then DEC, a net +1),\nbit 1 set means step left (DEC alone). Bit 0 is the complement of bit 1\nand is never tested -- they are flipped together by EOR #$03 -- so the\ndirection is really one bit written as two.\n\nIf the cell it wants is not empty, SnakeHeadChecksCell kills the player if\nit is the ship, and otherwise hands back the OLD column, so the head stays\nput; then it drops a row and reverses. Reaching row 22 wraps it back to\nrow 14, column 2, with the direction forced to right. Note it drops a row\nonly on a bounce, never on an open step -- so it zigzags across the whole\nwidth of the grid and the walls are the $20 cells outside the playfield." },
        { "id": "cmt_l7jho9", "address": "$8AA4", "text": "Killing a segment in the middle of the snake SPLITS it.\n\nIt walks backwards from the hit segment to the nearest one carrying the\nhead bit, takes that head flag word, and ORs it into the segment on the\nfar side of the gap -- which after RemoveSnakeSegment compacts the array\nis the one that inherits the hit segment index. So the tail half acquires\nits own head and starts moving independently instead of following a\nsegment that is no longer there. The segment before the gap gets bit 7 set\nso it is erased and redrawn properly.\n\nThis only runs for a plain body segment (flags & $C0 == 0). Killing the\nhead itself takes the other path at $8A8D, which promotes the next\nsegment; killing one already marked takes neither." },
        { "id": "cmt_8qhsl5", "address": "$8ECC", "text": "Second look at the two status-line glyph pairs, and one of them does resolve.\n\n$1D ($8EE8) is unambiguously a 6x5 letter H. $1E ($8EF0) is a serifed letter I at columns 0-3, plus two isolated 2x1 marks at columns 5-6 on rows 2 and 4 - a colon. So $0418/$0419, the pair immediately left of the high score, reads \"HI:\". That is the field label, in a small font that exists nowhere else in the charset.\n\nBy the same argument $19/$1A at $040C/$040D should be the score's label in the same style, and I still cannot read it. What is there: $19 has a closed bowl at columns 0-3 (####, ##.#, ####, ##.., ##..) which looks like P, and a two-pixel vertical at columns 6-7 running all five rows; $1A adds a matching vertical at columns 14-15 and a broken horizontal along row 5. Read as one 16x5 image it is a P followed by two uprights joined at the foot. I am recording that rather than picking a letter.\n\nWhat would settle it: run the cartridge and photograph row 0, or find another game using the same small font." },
        { "id": "cmt_7h85fj", "address": "$8020", "text": "Returns the character at (plotColumn, plotRow) like ReadScreenChar, but jumps to PlayerHit instead if it is the ship. Used by the horizontal zapper beam, which is the one thing that must kill on the step rather than one frame later." },
        { "id": "cmt_cz0v0q", "address": "$859D", "placement": "inline", "text": "X zapper and Y zapper are my names, not the program's: the one on column 0 fires along X, the one on row 22 along Y" },
        { "id": "cmt_g31l4y", "address": "$865A", "text": "The vertical bar is drawn over rows 21 down to 3 with no collision test at all -- if the player is under it the ship character is simply overwritten, and MovePlayer discovers that on the next pass through CheckLethalChar." },
        { "id": "cmt_i2o40d", "address": "$8CF4", "text": "Correction to the last line of the note at $8CB4: $8CF4 is the base byte of the THIRD table, not the first, and its value $19 is never read - levelNumber is 1-indexed and caps at $1F, so no table byte at offset 0 or above $1F is ever fetched." },
        { "id": "cmt_zn1qli", "address": "$86D0", "text": "Called only by UpdateZapperBeam, and it does two unrelated things: it advances the beam character and it steals a tick from the laser counter, which is how the beam paces itself without a counter of its own." },
        { "id": "cmt_d5ta6f", "address": "$821D", "placement": "inline", "text": "this CMP is the last thing to touch carry before the laser sound reads it - see $8573" },
        { "id": "cmt_g6oeuo", "address": "$8573", "text": "The laser sound: two noise voices retriggered every tick with pitches taken from laserSoundCounter, which counts down from $40, so the bolt makes a falling hiss that ends when the counter reaches zero.\n\nThe interesting instruction is ADC #$00 at $857C with no CLC in front of it, which makes voice 1's pitch laserSoundCounter PLUS THE INHERITED CARRY while voice 2 gets the counter exactly. Whether the two voices are detuned by one step therefore depends on the carry flag left by whatever ran last.\n\nIt is traceable, and it lands somewhere unexpected. Nothing between here and CheckPauseKey touches carry - LDA, STA, DEC, BNE, JSR, JMP and RTS all leave it alone - and MainLoop calls CheckPauseKey immediately before MainLoopBody, whose first call is UpdatePlayerLaser, whose first call is this. CheckPauseKey ends on CMP #$29 against $C5, the KERNAL last-key-pressed byte.\n\nSo: with no key held, $C5 is $40, $40 >= $29, carry is set, and the two voices are one step apart. Hold any key whose matrix code is below $29 and carry is clear and both voices get the same pitch.\n\nRun, not argued. run_block on $821B with $C5 = $40 exits C=1 and with $C5 = $10 exits C=0; run_block on $8578 with laserSoundCounter = $20 writes $D401 = $20 / $D408 = $1F when C=1, and $1F / $1F when C=0.\n\nWhat I cannot tell you is whether anyone ever hears it. One step at the top of the frequency-high byte is a real interval, and the beat between two detuned noise voices is exactly the sort of thing that gives a 1982 laser its texture - but establishing that it is audible needs the machine, not the listing." },
        { "id": "cmt_qtqkqw", "address": "$857C", "placement": "inline", "text": "no CLC: voice 1 gets counter+C, and C comes from CheckPauseKey CMP #$29 against $C5" },
        { "id": "cmt_q4j1dj", "address": "$8380", "text": "LDA then CMP #$00 -- the CMP is redundant, LDA already set Z. Skips the delay entirely when animRadius is zero, which is how the materialisation animation shortens as the ring closes in." },
        { "id": "cmt_q4fgv2", "address": "$863C", "placement": "inline", "text": "gated twice: laserTickCounter 5 AND beamActive $FF, so the stolen tick only happens while a beam is in flight" },
        { "id": "cmt_4s0g51", "address": "$8AE0", "text": "Every way the player can die, from the seven inbound references to PlayerHit. Four are live and three are in orphan fragments nothing reaches:\n\n  $8028 CheckPlayerAtCell - the zapper beam head walks onto the ship. Called from UpdateZapperBeam as it advances the horizontal head one cell.\n  $877E and $87B8 MoveOnePodDown - a falling pod is already on the ship's cell, or lands on it. Two sites because it checks before and after the move.\n  $8AEB SnakeHeadChecksCell - the snake head steps into the ship.\n  $8BF8 CheckLethalChar - the ship tries to move onto a cell that is not $00, $08 or $09, reached from MovePlayer.\n\n  $8AD9 and $8AF5 are in the dead fragments at $8AD4 and $8AF1 and are reached by nothing.\n\nTwo things follow. The vertical zapper bar is not fatal in itself - only the horizontal head that CheckPlayerAtCell tests is - so the ship survives being crossed by the bar. And an explosion cell never kills: IsExplosionChar checks the destination against explosionFrameChain first and, on a match, restores the ship's old position and returns, so walking into an explosion costs you the move and nothing else. The lethal set is genuinely small: the pod, the snake, and the beam head." },
        { "id": "cmt_tsoot3", "address": "$8989", "text": "A second dead stub, immediately after the one at $8984: LDX savedIndex / INX / CPX segmentCount / RTS. It walks the segment array upward where the live UpdateSnake walks it downward with DEX, so this and its neighbour look like the remains of an earlier version of that loop." },
        { "id": "cmt_7w6d55", "address": "$87D9", "text": "The rewind, and the evidence that it is the only way into chain entry 1.\n\nAfter DEX, X is 0..6. X=0 (the hit char was $18) takes the branch and\nclears the cell for 10 points. Otherwise it writes chain[X], which for\nX=1..6 is $18, $0D, $0E, $0F, $10, $11 -- so hitting $0D writes $18, and a\nfurther shot on that cell clears it.\n\nEnumerating every write of a character into the play area: PlotCharAndColour\ntakes plotChar, and the complete set of things stored into plotChar is $00,\n$01, $02, $07, $0F, $13, $16, $20, $3F, laserCharPhase ($08/$09),\nbeamCharPhase ($05/$06) and it minus two ($03/$04), snakeHeadChar\n($13-$15), debrisChar ($16-$18) and this instruction. Outside\nPlotCharAndColour there are only AdvanceExplosions, which writes chain[2..7]\n= $0D-$12, ExplosionBecomesPod, which writes $0A or $12, and MoveOnePodDown,\nwhich writes $00 and $0A.\n\n$18 appears in exactly two of those: here, and debrisChar during the player\ndeath animation -- which runs in its own loop with AdvanceExplosions not\nbeing called, and is wiped by ClearPlayArea afterwards. So during play, an\nexplosion never starts at $18; it always starts at $0F, and entry 1 exists\nonly to be the last step of a rewind." },
        { "id": "cmt_be9vp9", "address": "$8818", "text": "The title screen, in full, because it is only four writes and it is the whole front end.\n\nBootShowTitle is reached from CopyCharacterSet -> InitVideoAndScreenTables -> here, so it is the first thing on screen after reset. It draws the status row, then jumps to BootTitleEntry which falls into DrawTitleScreenText and DrawCopyrightRow. The result, by screen address:\n\n  row 0,  cols 0-39   the status row from statusLineChars: \"GRIDRUNNER\" (cyan then purple), a two-glyph mark, seven score digits, a second mark, seven high-score digits, the ship glyph, and the lives digit \"4\"\n  row 6,  cols 11-24  \"BY JEFF MINTER\" in cyan\n  row 8,  cols 11-24  \"ENTER LEVEL nn\" in white, the two digits at $0557/$0558 rewritten by PrintTitleLevelNumber\n  row 10, cols 3-34   \"(c) 1982 HES  PRESS FIRE TO BEGIN\" in yellow\n\nNothing else is drawn and nothing animates. TitleJoystickLoop then spins on $DC11: up raises the level and redraws the two digits, fire starts the game.\n\nThe high score persists across games because it lives in screen RAM at $041B-$0421 and the title screen never clears row 0 - ClearPlayfield starts at $0450. It is lost on reset like anything else in RAM." },
        { "id": "cmt_exkn2e", "address": "$886F", "text": "This byte, $04, is the 40th and last colour of the status row - the purple behind the lives digit at $0427. It is outside the statusLineColours region, which runs $8847-$886E: DrawStatusLine reads LDA statusLineChars+$0027,X with X = $28 down to 1, so the colours are $8848-$886F, one higher at both ends than the region says. The region is off by one in the same direction twice, so it has the right length and the wrong offset. Left for whoever declared it to move rather than nested over the top of it." },
        { "id": "cmt_k82ngl", "address": "$8727", "text": "Two things about this table that the three loops reading it make exact.\n\nAll three readers - IsExplosionChar $89AB, AdvanceExplosions $8701 and LaserHitExplosion $87CD - start LDX #$07 and end DEX / BNE, so the entries actually reached are 1 to 7: $18 $0D $0E $0F $10 $11 $12. Entry 0 at $871F is the $EA the search stops before, which is already noted.\n\nEntry 8, at $8727, is $13 - the snake body character - and no loop can reach it. X never gets to 8. So there is a ninth byte in this table that is the right kind of value for it, sitting one past every index that is used. Read it alongside the rest of the patch evidence (the NOP runs at $834E, the 32-slot pod table scanned as 24, the write-only flag bit at $1300): the shape of a chain that used to be one entry longer, and whose last entry was a snake segment.\n\nThat would have meant an explosion ending as a new snake segment rather than as a pod - which is what ExplosionBecomesPod does now. It is a guess about intent and nothing in the binary confirms it. What is certain is that $8727 is never read.\n\nSecond: $0A, the pod, is not in this table. LaserHitExplosion therefore never matches a pod, falls through to LaserHitSegment, which tests only $13/$14/$15 and then hits the three NOPs at $8A1D and returns normally - without the PLA PLA the other paths use and without setting laserRow to $FF. So a laser bolt passes straight through a pod: no score, no kill, and the cell is overwritten with $00 by the plot at $8538 until MoveOnePodDown draws the pod again on its next step. Pods are unshootable, and the only thing that removes one is its own fall off the bottom of the grid." },
        { "id": "cmt_mfsppl", "address": "$8A25", "text": "Scoring, complete. AddScore has four inbound references and one of them is its own loop, so there are exactly three ways to score in this game. X selects the digit, and with six live digits at $0410-$0415 that means X=6 is units, X=5 tens, X=4 hundreds.\n\n  $888E  AwardPodPointsAndSound, X=6 Y=$0A  ->    10   shooting an explosion out completely\n  $8AA1  ScoreAndSweep,          X=4 Y=1    ->   100   any snake segment destroyed\n  $8A25  LaserHitSegment,        X=4 Y=3    ->   300   extra, and only for a segment drawn as $14 or $15\n\nThe third one is worth following through, because it is a rule about the game and not about the code. The follower branch at $891D draws every body segment as $13, flat; only the head, committed at $896A, is drawn with snakeHeadChar, which cycles $13 -> $14 -> $15 -> $13. LaserHitSegment scores on the character it hit.\n\nSo: a body segment is always 100. The head is 100 when it happens to be showing $13 and 400 when it is showing $14 or $15 - which is two ticks in three. Shooting the head is worth up to four times a body segment, and the difference is decided by the animation frame it is on at the moment the bolt arrives.\n\nAnd RemoveSnakeSegment has four inbound references, all inside LaserHitSegment. Nothing else in the program can delete a segment: the zapper beam draws over cells without testing what was there, and its own collision check is CheckPlayerAtCell, which only looks for $07. The beam is a hazard to the player and a source of pods, and it is harmless to the snake." }
      ],
